  {"type": "split", "h2": "Will my ductwork work with a central heat pump?", "image": "Furnace with cased coil and supply plenum connected to existing ductwork in a utility room", "image_src": "/assets/img/work/furnace-coil-utility-room.webp", "image_fit": "cover", "image_credit": "EHI install", "image_pos": "50% 45%", "html": "<p>Usually yes, but we check rather than assume. A heat pump delivers air at roughly 35°C to 45°C instead of the 50°C to 60°C a gas furnace pushes out, so it moves more air to deliver the same heat. Ducts sized for a furnace sometimes can't move that air without high static pressure, which means noise, lost capacity and a shorter equipment life.</p><p>On the assessment visit we measure total external static pressure with a manometer and compare it to the equipment's rated limit (typically 0.5 inches of water column for residential air handlers). We look for undersized returns, crushed flex runs and leaky trunk joints. Most Ontario homes need nothing more than a larger return drop or a few sealed joints. Where ducts really are too small, we tell you the fix and the cost up front rather than let the system struggle.</p>"},
